From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jrash <johnrash@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 04:19:46 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 161/342] meson_vdec, meson_hdmi, MediaPlayer: show the
 decoder's frames without a copy

The hardware decoder's frames were copied into MediaPlayer's overlay
bitmaps, about 3 ms a 1080p frame into write-combining memory. Now the
decoder can write a reference to its frame into the bitmap instead, and
the overlay shows the frame where it is.

- MediaPlayer asks for this per frame (media_decode_info.codec_data, a
  video_frame_reference_request; VideoFrameReference.h), only for B_NV12
  while its decoder writes into the overlay's own bitmaps and the overlay
  is shown. Decoders that don't know the request ignore it.
- meson_decoder writes a meson_vdec_frame_reference (the frame area,
  offsets, rows, a check value) and keeps the frame until that buffer
  comes back to be written again, which means it is no longer shown; all
  held frames go back before the session stops. The environment variable
  MESON_DECODER_NO_REFERENCES turns this off.
- meson_hdmi takes a reference only for an area of the kernel's named
  "meson_vdec frame", physically contiguous, with the frame inside it,
  and points the video layer's canvases at the frame; chroma has its own
  offset now.
- The decoders' frame pools have three frames more (H.264: references + 8,
  HEVC: + 10), for the ones the screen holds.

MediaPlayer's CPU, share of all 4 cores over 10 s, copy against
reference: HEVC 1080p60 6.9% -> 1.8%, H.264 1080p60 5.4% -> 1.6%, HEVC
1080p30 3.9% -> 1.3%; the frame generator thread 5.5% -> 0.5% at HEVC
1080p60. HDMI recordings of a clip with its frame number in bit boxes
show every frame once, in order, at 60 fps, and each twice at 30 fps;
no tears; seeks, pause, a playlist and a window partly off screen work.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
